A||||5||Gokart||Stainless steel question||||5||
Z||000000||Gokart||07-05-2000||12:48 PM||||Removed battery tray today to grind down rust and wash acid residue my old battery left behind. When I put it back in I would like to use stainless steel screws. Will the tray be grounded using these? I know it must be grounded because of thin black wire that connects to tray. <P>------------------<BR> Marc                     87GT||||reg||5
Z||000001||lowCG||07-05-2000||04:22 PM||||Yes,if it was before,stainless fasteners won't change anything.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gokart||07-05-2000||08:30 PM||||Thanks! They weren't stainless before though. There GMs little bolts with built in washers that I'm so tired of dremeling off rust every year  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> Wasn't sure if stainless would make a ground the way a oem screw would. I wonder if "coated" stainless steel would be a problem though?<p>[This message has been edited by Gokart (edited 07-05-2000).]||||reg||7
Z||000003||hugh||07-05-2000||09:23 PM||||Stainless steel is definately the way to go.It won't corrode and if it is a ground it's electrical character is as good as any other metal. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||webbee||07-05-2000||10:05 PM||||Stainless is great. I try to find ss to replace anything except hardened bolts. It conducts electricity better as no corrosive build-up. It doesn't have to be coated. Takes paint just like steel. Go for it, but leave the ground wire.||||reg||14
Z||000005||Gokart||07-06-2000||05:51 AM||||Thanks again guys! Gosh I love those little drawers full of stainless hardware at True Vaule! Off I go <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
